  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1112 AM MDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nThe weather pattern will be somewhat less active this afternoon \nand evening as the large low pressure system near the Four Corners\nRegion lifts out of the area to the east. the Sub-tropical jet \nremains near the area and will continue to help generate isolated\nto scattered thunderstorm development over areas east of the Rio\nGrande late this afternoon and overnight. The most likely areas of\nstorm development will be in Otero, Hudspeth, El Paso Counties \nand locations to the east. There will be a few isolated \nthunderstorms in the afternoon, but the most active period will be\nlater in the evening after 9 pm when a short wave trough embedded\nin the flow pattern helps generate scattered strong thunderstorm \ndevelopment. The severe threat from storms will be reduced but \nstill possible. There is a potential for excessive rainfall with \nflash flooding from a few storms, but should not be as widespread \nas previous days. \n\nMonday should be a quieter day as the local forecast area will be\nwest of the trough axis with a drier, more stable mass moving into\nthe region. Short range models seem to reflect this with only a\nfew brief isolated storms for areas east of El Paso in the evening\nhours. Tuesday and Wednesday will be dry for southern New Mexico\nand Far West Texas as a dry westerly zonal flow sets up over the\nregion. Temperatures will be near seasonal in most areas. \n\nWe will return to a more active weather pattern Thursday and\nFriday as another trough sets up on the west coast and a ridge\nbuilds over the Gulf Coast. This pattern will allow moisture back\ninto the region that will help fuel scattered showers and\nthunderstorms across the area both days. \n\nThe outlook for Halloween on Saturday looks dry with a short wave\nridge moving over the state for a short period of quiet weather.\nActive weather returns to the area next Sunday as the trough on\nthe west coast deepens and moves inland toward New Mexico.",
